      "problem": "Consider a $3\\times 4$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}({A}^{T}))=2,$ then $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A))=$",
      "worked_solution": "The four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14247906/assets/889b83fadd26b377c4dd34143c9b16541630c04e90de3d9b005e7147ead75ad5.png)\n\nIn our case, we are given that $m=3,$ $n=4,$ and $m-r=2.$ \n\nSubstituting $m=3$ into $m-r=2,$ we can find the rank of $A:$\n\n$$\n(3)-r=2\\,⟹\\,r=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A)) & =r=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
